#=GF RA   Chi YI, Martick M, Lares M, Kim R, Scott WG, Kim SH
#=GF RL   PLoS Biol. 2008;6:e234.
#=GF RN   [5]
#=GF RM   26398724
#=GF RT   Two Divalent Metal Ions and Conformational Changes Play Roles in the
#=GF RT   Hammerhead Ribozyme Cleavage Reaction.
#=GF RA   Mir A, Chen J, Robinson K, Lendy E, Goodman J, Neau D, Golden BL
#=GF RL   Biochemistry. 2015;54:6369-6381.
#=GF RN   [6]
#=GF RM   26551631
#=GF RT   Two Active Site Divalent Ions in the Crystal Structure of the Hammerhead
#=GF RT   Ribozyme Bound to a Transition State Analogue.
#=GF RA   Mir A, Golden BL
#=GF RL   Biochemistry. 2016;55:633-636.
#=GF CC   The hammerhead ribozyme is one of the smallest catalytic RNAs. These RNAs
#=GF CC   have an endonuclease function, and most often are autocatalytic.
#=GF CC   Structurally it is composed of three base paired helices, separated by
#=GF CC   short linkers of conserved sequence. These helices are called I, II and
#=GF CC   III. We have classified hammerhead ribozymes into three types based on
#=GF CC   which helix the 5' and 3' ends of the sequence join. This family are the
#=GF CC   type III hammerheads. The conserved uridine-turn links helix I to helix II
#=GF CC   and usually has the sequence CUGA. Helix II and III are linked by a
#=GF CC   sequence CGAAA. The cleavage reaction occurs between helix III and I, and
#=GF CC   is usually a C. Hammerhead ribozymes are found in plant viroids and other
#=GF CC   small replicating satellite RNA species. Hammerhead ribozymes have been
#=GF CC   found in animals as well as plants. The structure of Hammerhead III
#=GF CC   ribozyme was reported in PDBs 2QUS, 2QUW [4], 5DI2, 5DI4, 5DQK [5], 5EAO,
#=GF CC   and 5EAQ [6].
#=GF WK   Hammerhead_ribozyme
#=GF SQ   85

